      "id": 1,
      "text": "Anchorage’s name comes from the estate of riverboat captain James W. Goslee, who donated an anchor from his ship to the town center.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        13,
        14,
        15
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Anchorage%2C_Kentucky"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "The city was incorporated in 1878, three years after Goslee’s death, and was originally known as Hite’s Mill and Hobbs Station.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        6,
        14
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Anchorage%2C_Kentucky"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "In 1901 an interurban rail line opened, linking Anchorage to Louisville and making it a summer retreat for wealthy Louisvillians.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        16,
        17
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Anchorage%2C_Kentucky"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "Papa John’s founder John Schnatter owns about 6% of Anchorage’s land, including much of the city center, and has pledged to preserve the city’s character.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        22,
